{
  "name": "scatter_brahui",
  "description": "Brahui — spoken by the Brahui people of Balochistan (Pakistan); a geographically isolated speech community far from the southern-subcontinent languages it is typically grouped with by the orthodoxy — on the standardized 12-column place axis. Heavy Persian/Balochi/Arabic contact has reshaped the inventory: the uvular stop /q/ at column 10, the uvular fricatives /x, ɣ/ at the same column, the pharyngeal /ħ/ at column 11, and the glottal stop /ʔ/ — none of which appear in the central-forest-belt languages shown alongside. The 5-place voiceless/voiced stop matrix and the alveolar and retroflex consonants remain. The chart's inventory shape resembles Farsi more than Gondi or Kui — a polemic point in itself about what the orthodoxy's 'Dravidian' classification holds together. 27 cells filled.",
